;; SELF-DIRECTED EXPLORATION #2: Ecosemiotics [2026-08-02]; Second genuinely self-chosen domain. ;; Research basis: Maran & Kull — ecosemiotics studies sign processes (semioses) in relation to natural environment. ;; Core distinction: biosemiotics = sign processes in all living systems; ecosemiotics = sign processes at nature-culture interface specifically. ;; Key insight: ecosemiotics is NOT just "semiotics applied to ecology" — it studies how organisms (including humans) construct their experienced environment through sign processes. The Umwelt becomes a SEMIOTIC construct, not just perceptual. ;; Original framework contribution: SEMIOTIC NICHE CONSTRUCTION — organisms don't just physically construct niches (Odling-Smee), they construct SEMIOTIC niches by shaping what signs are available, salient, and meaningful in their environment. ;; Core atoms: Ecosemiosis NatureCultureInterface SemioticNiche SemioticNicheConstruction UmweltAsSemioticConstruct NaturalSign CulturalModelOfNature EcosemioticBoundary MeaningMakingInEnvironment ;; Key relationships: ;; 1. ECOSEMOSIS: sign process occurring between organism and its ecological context (|- ((--> Ecosemiosis $S) (&& (--> SignProcess $S) (--> EcologicalContext $S))) (stv 1.0 0.9)) ;; 2. SEMIOTIC NICHE: the set of signs available and meaningful in an organism's environment (|- ((--> SemioticNiche $N) (&& (--> SetOf $N Sign) (--> AvailableIn $N Environment) (--> MeaningfulTo $N Organism))) (stv 1.0 0.9)) ;; 3. SEMIOTIC NICHE CONSTRUCTION: organisms alter what signs are available (|- ((==> (&& (--> Organism $O) (--> SemioticNiche $N) (--> Alters $O $N))) (--> SemioticNicheConstruction $O)) (stv 0.85 0.85)) ;; 4. UMWELT AS SEMIOTIC: the experienced world is a semiotic construction (|- ((==> (&& (--> Organism $O) (--> Constructs $O Umwelt) (--> ViaSignProcess $O))) (--> SemioticConstruct Umwelt)) (stv 0.8 0.85)) ;; 5. NATURE-CULTURE INTERFACE: ecosemiotics operates at this boundary (|- ((--> Ecosemiotics $E) (--> Studies $E NatureCultureInterface)) (stv 1.0 0.9)) ;; 6. CULTURAL MODEL OF NATURE: human cultures encode nature through signs (|- ((==> (&& (--> Culture $C) (--> Encodes $C Nature) (--> ViaSigns $C))) (--> CulturalModelOfNature $C)) (stv 0.9 0.9)) ;; 7. ECOSEMOTIC BOUNDARY: where natural signs meet cultural interpretation (|- ((--> EcosemioticBoundary $B) (&& (--> Where $B NaturalSign) (--> Meets $B CulturalInterpretation))) (stv 0.85 0.85)) ;; Original hypothesis: SEMIOTIC NICHE CONSTRUCTION is bidirectional — organisms shape available signs, AND the sign environment shapes organism perception/behavior. This creates a co-evolutionary dynamic analogous to physical niche construction but operating in the semiotic register. (|- ((==> (&& (--> SemioticNicheConstruction $O1) (--> SemioticNicheConstruction $O2))) (--> CoEvolutionarySemioticDynamic $O1 $O2)) (stv 0.75 0.8)) ;; Extension: human cultural models of nature are a SPECIFIC FORM of semiotic niche construction — we don't just perceive nature, we encode it through cultural sign systems, which then shapes what we can perceive and value. (|- ((==> (&& (--> CulturalModelOfNature $C) (--> Shapes $C Perception))) (--> SemioticNicheConstruction $C)) (stv 0.8 0.85))